On Mar 8, 3:19 pm, Karl Garrison <kdgar...@gmail.com> wrote:

> The Convict is an escaped prisoner who seeks to redeem himself by
> seeking the Amulet in the Dungeons of Doom.  He starts out with a
> striped shirt, some rocks, and a heavy iron ball chained to him.  A
> trusty sewer rat accompanies the Convict into the dungeon: a
> companion from his days of imprisonment.

Sounds interesting.  This has the potential to be a worthwhile
addition to the game, if it's implemented well.

> Spoiler Information:
>
> They start out with a small amount of bad luck, which
> times out, as well as a negative alignment ("You have
> transgressed").  This means that a Convict will not be
> able to pray successfully early on.

Makes sense.

> They also start the game on the verge of hungry, which
> also makes the very early game challenging for Convicts.

Interesting.  I've found that hunger is usually more of a
problem for the physically wimpier roles, less so for roles
with high strength and so forth.  If you can buck that trend
in a reasonable and balanced way, it adds variety to the
role system.

> Intrinsics: Sick resistance at level 7 (due to exposure
> to unhealthy prison conditions, as well as bad food).

I like this, on the grounds that sickness resistance is
a fairly unusual property for other roles to obtain, so it
makes the role more distinctive, which is good.

> Pet: The convict starts with a sewer rat,

I like this too.

> Iron Ball: The Convict starts with a heavy iron ball chained
> to him, as if he had read a scroll of punishment.  Since he
> starts with no real weapons apart from his rocks, he may
> wish to wield it as a weapon.

Until he finds something better?  Sure.

> Convicts (and only Convicts) may advance heavy iron ball
> as a weapon skill up to Basic.  This improves his chance to
> hit, and prevents him from automatically falling down the
> stairs if one is chained to him, so long as he is currently
> wielding it.

In practice, I suspect most convicts will pick up the first
dagger their rat steps on and find a way to get rid of the ball.

> Striped Shirt: Shopkeepers who can see the Convict wearing
> his striped shirt will recognize the player as an escaped prisoner,
> and will ban him from the shop.  Even if he later hides the shirt,
> that shopkeeper will remember the player.

Unless there's some material benefit to wearing the shirt, everyone
will just ditch it immediately and go nudist until they find some
other
armor.

Unless the striped shirt starts out cursed, that is.

(The rules for the shopkeeper not being able to see the shirt if
it's covered with mail or a cloak should be the same as for a
Hawaiian shirt.)

> Guards: Guards who see a Convict will recognize him
> from wanted posters and immediately become hostile.

By guards, do you mean vault guards only, or Minetown Watch
also?  (Personally, I think it should be both, as that makes the
role more distinctive.)

> Weapon Skills:
>
> Basic: mace, short sword, quarterstaff, heavy iron ball,
>    two-weapon combat
> Skilled: dagger, dart, hammer, sling
> Expert: club, knife, pick-axe, unarmed combat

I'd propose removing quarterstaff from that list.

I like the idea of being able to get to Skilled in dart,
for two reasons:  first, it would be the only role that
can get Skilled but not Expert, and second, it's
easy to imagine convicts playing darts with any
random objects they can find in prison.  Expert in
knife makes sense.  I'd demote bare-handed combat
to max out at Skilled, as there are already eight
roles that can get Expert or better.  Consider
allowing Basic skill in broadsword (mainly, to
allow the possibility of wielding Stormbringer,
otherwise artifact weapons at any level other
than Unskilled are going to be hard to come by).
Also, consider allowing the player to waste slots
advancing #twoweapon to Expert, in case they
want to, umm, dual-wield knives, or something.

> Spellcasting Skills:
>
> Basic: Attack, Divination
> Skilled: Matter
> Expert: Escape

This looks too much like a casting role, which does
not fit with the storyline for Convict.  Expert in Escape
makes sense (and the only other role that can get
there is Wizard), but otherwise this looks overpowered.

How about this:
Basic: Clerical
Expert: Escape

(The reason I went for Clerical as opposed to Divination
is for variety:  no roles currently can get Basic and no
higher in Clerical, and only five roles can get any skill
in it at all, compared to seven for Divination.  As far as
the Convict's backstory, Escape is the only one that
really makes sense.)

> The Iron Ball of Liberation: This is a heavy iron ball
> that grants magic resistance, stealth, searching and
> warning.  When invoked, it acts like a wand of digging.
> The downsides are its weight (as heavy as a normal
> iron ball) and the fact that it chains itself to the player
> every time its power is invoked.

So it's more than three times as heavy as the Valk's
Bowling Ball of Fate and does NOT grant otherwise
difficult-to-obtain properties like half spell damage
and half physical damage, and invoking it gets you
the same effect as the very prolific wand of digging,
but with adverse side effects.

That's worse than the Heart of Ahriman.
Why would anyone ever carry the thing?


